<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bahurutshe Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1852 CE (approx.) · capital Kaditshwene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior house of the Tswana, whose stone-walled capital Kaditshwene impressed early 19th-century visitors as one of the largest towns in southern Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahurutshe">Bahurutshe</a> trace their descent from the founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana</a> lineage, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a> and other groups later branched. By the 16th century they were settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marico-river">Marico</a> district of the western highveld, where they built aggregated towns with stone-walled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> enclosures.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs ruled large towns divided into wards, each under a headman, with public assemblies (kgotla) for justice and debate. The economy combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding, cereal farming and skilled metalworking in iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. When the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-campbell-missionary">John Campbell</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaditshwene">Kaditshwene</a> in 1820 he estimated its population at 16,000 and admired its metal crafts and trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaditshwene">Kaditshwene</a> was destroyed in the wars of the early 1820s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahurutshe">Hurutshe</a> later suffered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Mzilikazi's Ndebele</a>. Reassembled under Moilwa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marico-river">Marico</a> valley, they came under the rule of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-african-republic">Transvaal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republic after 1852, though Hurutshe communities persist around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeerust">Zeerust</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1852 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1852 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaditshwene</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moilwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tswana religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hurutshe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1676&amp;focus=bahurutshe-chiefdom" aria-label="Bahurutshe Chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5374 3111 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bahurutshe Chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5694 3367L5746 3380"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5746 3380h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5694 3367h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5694 3367h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kaditshwene. <a href="/atlas?year=1676&amp;focus=bahurutshe-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

